This has ideas about porting Super ZZ Zero to other systems. It also
includes notes about possible new or changed user configuration options.

The SDL data types are used in many files, but the SDL functions should be
used only in audio.c and display.c; they may be changed to not use SDL.

In some cases it might help to add the changes into this repository, and
in some cases it might be better for someone to maintain a separate
repository for these changes; it is also possible to do some of both, in
case that can help for some kind of changes.


=== SDL2, SDL3, etc ===

Compatibility layers exist, but its use may need testing. Some below
functions would not be implemented if compatibility layer is used.

joy_gamecontroller
  Set to 1 to use the Game Controller functions. If this is set, then the
  button numbers will use the SDL_GameControllerButton enumeration.

joy_gamecontroller_mapping
  If defined, then it will call SDL_GameControllerAddMapping with the
  specified string as the argument. See SDL documentation for details.

joy_light
  Allows specifying the conditions for controlling the LEDs of joypads, if
  possible. For example, it can be set to change the light according to the
  game speed or if the game is paused or if a menu is displayed.

screen_saver
  If set to 0 or 1, disables or enables the screen saver. Set it to 2 to
  disable the screen saver when the game is running but enable in the
  editor and when the game is paused.

video_opacity
  Can be used to set the opacity of the window. This is a floating number,
  where 0 means fully transparent and 1 means fully opaque. This might not
  be supported on all systems.

There can be a [SDL_HINT] division for setting SDL configuration hints.
Note that not all of the hints are meaningful for Super ZZ Zero.

The use of config.text_input also might need to be changed.


=== GNU/Linux ===

Super ZZ Zero is already designed for GNU/Linux systems, although it might
be helpful to be included in package managers of some distributions.

Some systems may use SDL2 or SDL3 instead of SDL1, especially if the system
is using Wayland. Compatibility layers exist, but its use may need testing.
See the above section about SDL for details.

It might also be possible in future to support some X window system
functions directly (e.g. use of primary and secondary selection, use of
non-Unicode text, etc). (Note that some versions of SDL allow use of
selections and clipboard but only support Unicode. For non-Unicode text,
it will be necessary to use the X window functions directly.)


=== BSD ===

Working on BSD is probably less difficult than DOS and Windows and other
systems since it is a UNIX system, although there are differences between
BSD and GNU and Linux.

The GNU functions and Linux functions used will need to be found and to be
changed to BSD functions. Lists of some of these are below, but they are
probably incomplete.

GNU functions:

* fopencookie is used; I think on BSD you would use funopen instead.

Linux functions:

* /proc/self/exe is used by the editor to call the executable in the game
play mode; I think on BSD you would use /proc/curproc/file instead but I
do not have BSD and cannot test this.


=== DOS ===

DOS has 8.3 case-insensitive file names. This should not be a problem with
most of the working of Super ZZ Zero.

The save/restore game menu might have to be changed somewhat, but still
mostly retaining the existing UI, to work with DOS file names properly.

The user configuration file will need to have a different name, and will
probably be stored in the same directory as the executable file.

audio_music_driver
  Specify the sound driver for music lumps. (It can also read settings
  from the BLASTER environment variable.)

audio_pc_driver
  Specify the sound driver for PC sound effects (#PLAY and SFX), such as
  using the PC speaker directly (which will disable the volume controls),
  or emulated using some sound card.

audio_wave_driver
  Specify the sound driver for wave sounds.

boss_key
  Disable or enable the "boss key" function.

copy_current_font
  If set to 1 then it enables the function to allow copying the current
  text mode font into the world file. This only works if the video mode
  is set to a suitable text mode.

external_port
  Works like "external" but with a serial port, or with some other suitable
  device if a suitable DOS device driver is loaded. You can specify which
  serial port, and optionally can also specify the baud rate to use.

help
  In DOS, if this option is not specified, it will look in the subdirectory
  of the directory with the executable file. (You can still override it
  with your own specification if you want to do.)

joy_calibration
  Sets the calibration setting for joysticks.

joy_type
  Specifies the joystick type, which can be "PC" (only two buttons and two
  axes), "GRAVIS" (has four buttons), or "GRAVIS_PRO" (which has many more
  buttons as well, and up to four controllers). The maximum joy_index that
  is allowed depends on the joystick type.

printer_port
  Specify the port that the printer is connected to, such as "LPT1". (If
  it is connected to a PostScript printer, you must use the printer_begin
  option to specify the name of a file containing a ESC/P emulator written
  in PostScript, and printer_end with whatever is used to terminate it.)
  Specify "FILE:" and a file name if you want to print to file.

show_status
  This setting is only meaningful with some video modes; it is not
  meaningful in text mode.

video_mode
  Specifies preferred and acceptable video modes.

The following configuration settings are not meaningful on DOS:
  full_screen
  joy_name
  text_input
  time_utc
  video_gamma

The [Emulator] division also will have to work differently; many things
will be built-in and use real sound cards if they are available, so it is
not necessary to load an external emulator specified in this division. (A
TSR also might be used for some kinds of sound card emulation.)


=== Windows ===

Windows also has case-insensitive file names like DOS has; this probably
does not require any changes to Super ZZ Zero. However, another feature
of file names in Windows is that they will normally use Unicode; this
will not work with Super ZZ Zero unless native_file_picker is used (see
the below description), but file names with only ASCII characters can
still be displayed and used. If the PC character set is in use (if the
current code page is 437), then it can be used directly.

It will probably also be appropriate on Windows to allow user settings to
be stored in the registry. Windows registry has several data types. For
Super ZZ Zero, different options have different types (although the text
configuration file always stores them as text). REG_SZ and REG_EXPAND_SZ
would be suitable for file names and for other text that is used with
Windows API functions. REG_DWORD (and sometimes REG_QWORD) would be used
for integer values and for boolean values. Other settings would probably
use REG_BINARY, but would also allow REG_SZ and REG_EXPAND_SZ if the string
is limited to only ASCII characters.

Windows also has file associations in the Explorer and File Manager, and
allows multiple menu options, so it can include menus for "play" and for
"edit", so that if a file is right-clicked you can select the function.

There is also COM automation, which also might have some use.

A built-in option menu for configuration by GUI might also be suitable.

auto_suffix
  On Windows, the default setting is 1 instead of 0. However, the meaning
  of this setting is the same as on Linux, even though the default value
  is different.

joy_guid
  Specifies the DirectX instance GUID for the joystick.

menu_bar
  Enables or disables the menu bar. You cannot push F10 to select the menu
  bar, but you can push ALT by itself to select the menu bar, if the menu
  bar is enabled.

native_file_picker
  If set to 1 then the save/restore game menu will use the Windows file
  dialog boxes instead of the built-in UI of Super ZZ Zero.

native_midi
  Set to 1 to use the Windows MIDI functions if a .BGM lump requests a
  sound card that uses MIDI but the emulator is not loaded.

native_text_edit
  If set to 1 then the text editing widget of Windows is used instead of
  the built-in UI of Super ZZ Zero.

printer_type
  Specify W to display the Windows printing dialog box when F11 is pushed.
  This will then be used to print using the functions of Windows.

renderer
  Specifies the type of renderer to use.

registry_key
  This setting is only effective if it is a command-line argument. If it is
  set to the path of a registry key, it will read the settings from the
  specified registry key (which you must have permission to read); if the
  menu is enabled then any changed settings are written to this registry
  key if you have permission to write to that registry key.

text_input
  Mode 1 is not meaningful, although there may be a new mode 3 for use with
  the non-Unicode functions of Windows.

time_format
  If not specified, it uses the date/time format setting in Windows.

time_format_mode
  Selects to use short or long date/time formats. (This setting is not
  meaningful if time_format is defined.)

The [Printer] division may be used for printer settings that are specific
to Windows that may be set per application program.


=== Mac OS X ===

I do not know much about it. However, there are a few things. The command
key is not used in Super ZZ Zero, but an implementation on Mac OS X can
use it for the common functions that it is used for with its combinations,
such as Command+Q to quit, Command+P to print, etc.

Mac OS X also uses UTI. The UTI for a Super ZZ Zero world file should be
"org.zzo38computer.superzz0.world", and this conforms to the UTI for the
Hamster archive format, which is "com.hamsterrepublic.lumped".

Some functions of Windows such as a configuration menu would also be
possible on Mac OS X, too. (On Mac OS X, the menu bar is always displayed,
unlike on Windows that you could enable or disable the menu bar.)

AppleScript is another thing; it might be possible to make it possible
to work some functions (e.g. board editor automation) with AppleScript,
but because it uses Unicode, it might not be possible to work properly
with non-Unicode text.

audio_plugin
  Specifies a Audio Units plugin to use.

native_file_picker
  Like on Windows.

native_text_edit
  Like on Windows.

text_input
  Mode 1 is not applicable.

time_format_mode
  Selects to use short or long date/time formats.


=== A currently unnamed operating system design ===

Command, Automation, and Query Language may be usable to command, automate,
and query the Super ZZ Zero.

The .CCM lumps would probably be used with this system, since they can be
used to interoperate text with other programs; it would also be usable to
allow the application to tell the keyboard manager which input character
set it wants, to automatically set the IME by the context. (This is not
possible in most other systems since they use Unicode, but this one uses
TRON code, which is better.)

In this system, many settings that refer to files and directories will
not work as is; the values will be links to the files instead. Settings
that refer to external programs will need to refer to capabilities. For
directories (only "help" so far), the link would usually be to a file with
a key/value list, which associates the names (in ASCII) to the files.

full_screen
  This is not available. If you want to use full screen, you must use the
  facilities of the operating system to display it as full screen, which
  is a separate setting from the application-specific configuration.

joy_index
  This is the zero-based player number of the joypad. (This will probably
  be zero; the operating system will automatically map the correct one,
  so it should not normally be necessary to specify any other numbers.)

native_text_edit
  Like on Windows.

printer_type
  This can be set to W to use the operating system's printing functions.
  In this case, printer_command may be set to a capability to use for
  printing; if printer_command is not set, then the capabilities passed
  to the program when it starts will be used.

text_input
  Mode 0 and 1 are not applicable, but there may be a new mode 4; you can
  then use mode 2 or 4.

time_format
  If not specified, the locale setting will be used (which also makes it
  possible to use non-Gregorian calendars), if it can be used with the
  current character set. This setting is ignored if Super ZZ Zero does
  not have permission to determine the current date and time.

time_format_mode
  Selects to use short or long date/time formats, according to the locale
  setting. (This setting is not meaningful if time_format is defined.)

The following configuration settings are not meaningful:
  auto_suffix
  file_list
  full_screen
  joy_name
  save_dir
  temporary_file_1
  temporary_file_2

(Such things as joypad selections and full screen are handled by other
software on the system and does not need to be configured by the
application programs, so such settings are not used by Super ZZ Zero.
The button/axis mapping still needs to be configured in Super ZZ Zero.)


=== HTML ===

One possible feature is to allow automation of some editor functions using
JavaScripts (like it would be possible using AppleScript on Mac OS X).

external_ws
  Specifies a URL of a web socket to use; a single DER item is received or
  sent in one message. This otherwise works like the "external" option.

joy_name
  When using HTML, the joy_name is the "id" property of the Gamepad object.
  If it does not match any of them exactly, it is a prefix.

joy_sensitivity
  On HTML, this is a floating point number rather than integer, but other
  than that, it works in the same way like it normally does.

js_time_format
  Specifies the JSON data which will be decoded and used as the options for
  the Intl.DateTimeFormat. (This will not work within the user interface of
  Super ZZ Zero itself unless the resulting format uses only ASCII
  characters, but can still be used in other interfaces.)

native_text_edit
  Like on Windows.

text_input
  Mode 1 is not applicable.

time_format
  If this contains non-ASCII characters, then they will work within the
  user interface of Super ZZ Zero, but not other HTML-based interfaces.

The following configuration settings are not meaningful on HTML:
  auto_suffix
  external
  extra_lump_file
  extra_lump_name
  file_list
  help
  printer_begin
  printer_command
  printer_end
  printer_type
  save_dir
  temporary_file_1
  temporary_file_2
  test_mode
  text_editor_command


=== RetroArch ===

Probably, if it is implemented on RetroArch then it will exclude the
editor, and only the game engine will be implemented.

Many configuration settings of Super ZZ Zero can have default values using
the mechanisms of RetroArch, so that they do not need to be set separately,
but in some cases it might be useful to override them.

RetroArch is intended to use the Super Nintendo game controls rather than
keyboard, but also works with keyboard. In Super ZZ Zero, some functions
will require the keyboard, and it might also depend on the game world; you
can define joystick mapping but not everything can be used with it.

Due to the use of the game control input in RetroArch, the joystick mapping
configuration can use the RetroArch button mapping names.

The RETROK_ constants in RetroArch seem to have all of the same values as
the SDLK_ constants in SDL1, so this will be easy to port. However, the
values for the modifier keys are different, and not compatible with SDL.

Super ZZ Zero can run at multiple speeds and allows the user to change the
speed although the game itself cannot change it, the game is able to define
the default speed. Due to this, it might be suitable to set the frame rate
to that specified by the game world (or the Super ZZ Zero configuration if
the game world does not specify or the user deliberately overrides it), but
the fast-forward functions of RetroArch can be used as an alternative way
to affect the game speed.

RETRO_ENVIRONMENT_GET_AUDIO_VIDEO_ENABLE: This may be used for optimization
since audio and video do not always need to be rendered. Super ZZ Zero does
not require screen capture nor audio recording.

RETRO_ENVIRONMENT_GET_LANGUAGE: May be used in future if game-specific user
preferences are implemented; one of the OIDs might be assigned for the
RetroArch language codes. The user may still override the user preferences
in which case the RetroArch language is not used for this purpose. However,
this function might also be used for text with Super ZZ Zero itself (e.g.
save game file pickers). Another possible use of this is in case it is not
possible for the front end to switch the character set to something other
than Unicode, the language can be used to guess at the proper way to
convert characters to/from TRON code.

RETRO_ENVIRONMENT_GET_LOG_INTERFACE: This might be used instead of stderr
for debugging messages (controlled by config.debug and config.music_debug).

RETRO_ENVIRONMENT_GET_MIDI_INTERFACE: Might be used as a built-in sound
chip emulator when MIDI is requested by a .BGM lump. This should be
configurable; it might use an external emulator like normal Super ZZ Zero.

RETRO_ENVIRONMENT_GET_SAVE_DIRECTORY: May be used as the default directory
for save games, if not defined using the Super ZZ Zero configuration.

RETRO_ENVIRONMENT_SET_MEMORY_MAPS: See retro_get_memory_data; in addition
to the ones specified there, there may be additional memory maps for the
board grid and possibly other stuff.

RETRO_ENVIRONMENT_SET_MESSAGE_EXT: The Super ZZ Zero message scrollback
might use RETRO_MESSAGE_TARGET_LOG, and the status indicators which are
normally displayed outside of the game video area would be set using the
RETRO_MESSAGE_TYPE_STATUS message type. Speed is handled by the front end
and so probably does not need to be displayed in this way.

RETRO_ENVIRONMENT_SET_PROC_ADDRESS_CALLBACK: This may be used for functions
that RetroArch does not support, such as printers and setting character
encoding (for message logging and file names and keyboard input).

RETRO_ENVIRONMENT_SET_SERIALIZATION_QUIRKS: Super ZZ Zero currently assumes
that save games are written to a file, and does not save the state of the
random number generator (which is not guaranteed to be consistent between
different versions of Super ZZ Zero, with the exception of the random
number generator used for item randomization). Using the serialization
function of RetroArch might not work as well as it should be, so some of
these quirks might apply, or possibly the serialization cannot be used
at all. (The current implementation of Super ZZ Zero is also limited that
you cannot save the game while a text window is open.)

RETRO_ENVIRONMENT_SET_SUPPORT_ACHIEVEMENTS: See retro_get_memory_data.

retro_get_memory_data: RETRO_MEMORY_SYSTEM_RAM would be used for the
Super ZZ Zero "program memory", RETRO_MEMORY_SAVE_RAM for high scores and
achievements (but not for save games; this is handled differently), and
RETRO_MEMORY_VIDEO_RAM for the emulated PC text mode. Super ZZ Zero does
not use the date/time for anything that affects the game (it is only used
for the save/restore game menu), so RETRO_MEMORY_RTC is not applicable.

retro_get_region: Probably should return RETRO_REGION_NTSC. However, some
games may specify PAL frame rates.

retro_load_game: This function is intended for loading a ROM image file,
but can also be used to load the .sz0 world file. The full path is not
required, although seeking is required, so I don't know if "need_fullpath"
should be required or not (the documentation says that it may be read from
stdin if the full path is not used; however, softpatching is useful).

Some of the above is probably wrong; someone who is more familiar with the
programming and usage of RetroArch might know better than I do about it.


=== Flatpak ===

I think Flatpak has many problems, but nevertheless someone may wish to
port this program to Flatpak for the benefit of people who do use Flatpak.

It expects a applicaion icon, but this program does not have one.

It also uses D-Bus, but I think that D-Bus has many problems, so I do not
use it in my programs, instead preferring other methods which do not use
and expect Unicode, and do not have the other problems of D-Bus.

Super ZZ Zero does not use XDG directories, although the program could be
modified to support this as a conditional compilation option, or a fork
that can be maintained if needed.

Audio permission is not required, although it should be specified as a
permission that it optionally uses.

For editing, the sz0asm and sz0music programs will also be helpful, but
those are command-line programs and there is no use to have icons and
desktop files and message bus etc for those.

File system access is used for:

* User configuration file (normally ~/.superzz0rc but a version for use
with Flatpak might use the XDG configuration directory instead). This is
only read and not written by Super ZZ Zero.

* World files, which are read-only for the game but writable in the editor.
(Only the world file specified as the command-line argument is accessed.)

* Save game files, which are only those specified by the user, in the user
configuration file and/or by the F3 and F4 menus. (These could be changed
to use portals, similar to what is described above for some of the other
ports to other systems.)

(In future, there might also be a high score file, which will also need to
be accessible unless the user disables the high score file.)

However, there is also the use of popen with commands specified by the user
at run time and in configuration file. The program can work without these
features, although then some features will be unavailable (such as the
ability to import/export parts of the world in the editor; you can still
use external programs to do this, though).

Also, the Super ZZ Zero editor will call itself using /proc/self/exe and I
don't know whether or not that works with Flatpak.

There is also sound card emulation, which are dynamic libraries loaded by
the user configuration file (and these dynamic libraries might do their own
file system access and other access). This feature is not required, but it
can be helpful if full functionality is desired.

There are a few ways of handing sound card emulation. One of these would be
to include all of them inside of the application package, so that they are
the only ones available, and the user configuration file will only select
them if multiple implementations are available and to configure them.


=== ScummVM ===

In case someone wanted to add the Super ZZ Zero game play only (without the
editor) into ScummVM.

ScummVM has its own sound card emulation, so these can be mapped to the
OIDs used by Super ZZ Zero, making that part of the Super ZZ Zero user
configuration file unnecessary. (This includes support for MIDI; in the
case of MIDI, it would also sometimes be possible to use MIDI mapping.)

Settings of Super ZZ Zero could be made into the engine-specific settings
of ScummVM, although some settings are not needed because the general
settings of ScummVM would be applicable instead, such as many of the audio
settings (sample rate, buffer size, etc).

Additionally, the settings specified by the [Special] division are specific
to each game that uses them (not all of them do), although the specific
game itself will include a menu for changing these settings, if applicable.

